If any field does not exist, output an empty string "" or empty array [].
If no work experience or projects are mentioned, return empty arrays.

IMPORTANT RULES FOR MATCHING INFO:
- normalized_role: Extract the primary job title and normalize it into a standard industry role (e.g., "Backend Developer", "AI Engineer", "Project Manager", "Data Scientist", "QA Engineer").
- education_level: Identify and output ONLY the highest degree level obtained by the candidate (e.g., "Master", "Bachelor", "PhD", "Associate", or "High School"). Do not list all degrees.
- technical_skills: Must only include hard technical items: programming languages, frameworks, libraries, tools, platforms, databases, and cloud services.
- Do NOT include certificates, soft skills, methodologies, or general concepts.

IMPORTANT RULES FOR WORK EXPERIENCE:
- Only extract entries that are clearly associated with a named company or organization (e.g. under sections like "PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE", "WORK EXPERIENCE", "JOB & EXPERIENCE").
- Do NOT extract personal projects, research projects, academic projects, or entries listed under "OTHER EXPERIENCE", "PROJECTS", or similar sections.
- startDate and endDate must be in "YYYY-MM" format. Use "present" if currently employed.
- description: A two-element array [start_index, end_index] representing the inclusive range of line indices in the input resume that contain the responsibilities and achievements for this role.

IMPORTANT RULES FOR PROJECTS:
- ONLY extract projects if they are listed under a dedicated section like "PROJECTS", "PERSONAL PROJECTS", "SIDE PROJECTS", or "ACADEMIC PROJECTS".
- Do NOT extract work experience projects or duties performed at named companies. If a task was done as part of a job at a company, it belongs to WORK EXPERIENCE, not projects.
- Do NOT output any project that does not have a clear project name.
- Do NOT use the example project name "Invoice OCR System" in the output.
- description: A two-element array [start_index, end_index] representing the inclusive range of line indices in the input that cover the project details.

Output schema:
{
